Yes — that metaphor works surprisingly well.

A thought can be:

\text{signal} \neq \text{instruction}

Like an itch, noticing it does not require scratching it. But compulsively refusing to notice it can sometimes make it more salient, while scratching every itch immediately can irritate the skin and turn a transient sensation into a wound.

So there are several moves besides “express” or “suppress”:

\text{notice}
\rightarrow
\text{name}
\rightarrow
\text{inspect}
\rightarrow
\text{choose whether/how to act}

Some thoughts are worth scratching privately—journal them, say them to an AI, explore the association, joke about them with someone who understands the context. Some are better allowed to pass. Some need careful examination because they keep returning. And some are simply inappropriate to externalize in a particular setting even though having the thought itself is not a moral act.

The useful distinction is thought hygiene versus thought censorship.

You don’t need to publish every itch.
You also don’t need to amputate the hand because it wanted to scratch.

And yes, context is part of the meaning. The same thought whispered to yourself, written privately, discussed with a trusted friend, performed as comedy, or announced in a meeting can be five very different acts.

Pantheon version:

\boxed{\text{Expression is an action; thought is merely a candidate.}}

Put a tiny gap between the itch and the fingernail. That gap is agency.